## Phase 1 - Implement definitions, snapshots, and open-session types

- [x] Add `context_menu/definition.lua` with documented
      `ContextMenuDefinition` and `ContextMenuEntry` annotations.
- [x] Support an optional non-empty title after validation.
- [x] Support menu-level optional `fg` and `bg` colors.
- [x] Validate every supplied `fg` and `bg` as an integer display color in the
      inclusive `COLOR_BLACK`..`COLOR_WHITE` range; reject `COLOR_RESET`,
      pens, and every non-color representation.
- [x] Require a non-empty `entries` list.
- [x] Require every entry to contain a non-empty string `label`.
- [x] Require every entry to contain a Lua function `on_select` for which
      `type(on_select) == 'function'`; reject callable tables, userdata, and
      every other value.
- [x] Support independent optional entry-level `fg` and `bg` colors.
- [x] Reject unknown shapes and invalid values with errors that identify the
      offending field and entry index.
- [x] Resolve missing colors through entry, menu, and system defaults without
      mutating the caller's table.
- [x] Make definition validation return a DwarfUI-owned copy containing the
      title, resolved pens, labels, and handler references.
- [x] Store that validated copy at registration and replace it only through
      re-registration or an explicit update operation.
- [x] Copy the stored validated definition into an open-session snapshot when
      the menu opens.
- [x] Ensure caller mutation after registration affects neither the current
      registration nor an open session.
- [x] Add documented target and anchor descriptor types in
      `context_menu/target.lua`.
- [x] Define a documented selection-context object containing target kind,
      anchor kind, numeric registration identity, captured screen position,
      a guaranteed-live resolved source, and copied map position where
      applicable.
- [x] Allocate a monotonically increasing numeric registration identity for
      each new widget or map registration. Re-registration and explicit update
      retain it; unregister followed by register receives a new identity.
- [x] Store source widgets, map owners, and source roots as weak references in
      an open session; resolve them only when validating the session or
      constructing the callback context.
- [x] Store only the numeric registration identity in a session's stable target
      descriptor; never retain a widget, owner, or map handle strongly as
      identity.
- [x] Define callback-context behavior when a weak source has disappeared:
      close without constructing a callback context or invoking the handler.
      Whenever `on_select` runs, its context's `source` is non-nil and live.
- [x] Never retain a native map object pointer when a copied coordinate or
      stable numeric identity is sufficient.
- [x] Add a documented open-session type that owns exactly one definition
      snapshot, target descriptor, anchor descriptor, and lifecycle state.
- [x] Add focused definition tests under `tests/context_menu/` for valid,
      invalid, empty, partially colored definitions, and rejection of callable
      non-function values.
- [x] Add focused snapshot tests proving caller mutation after registration or
      opening alters neither the next opening nor the active menu.

### Phase 1 implementation record

- `dwarfui/context_menu/definition.lua` owns strict definition validation,
  resolved display-color pens, atomic registration-owned definition slots, and
  structurally isolated opening snapshots. Snapshot, resolved-entry, and
  resolved-pen constructors also serve as copy constructors.
- `dwarfui/context_menu/target.lua` owns immutable target, anchor, and session
  enums; monotonic registration identity allocation; copied stable
  descriptors whose constructors also serve as copy constructors; weak source
  ownership; live selection contexts; and close-before-callback session
  behavior.
- Reusable numeric classification and immutable numeric enum construction live
  in `dwarfui/utils/numbers.lua` and
  `dwarfui/utils/immutable_enum.lua`; context-menu modules do not redefine
  those helpers locally.
- The new modules are registered in dependency order and are included in the
  package-contract inventory. Focused definition/session tests live under
  `tests/context_menu/`.
- Verification on 2026-07-30: Lua 5.4.6 syntax passed for 28 production and
  47 test/support files; focused context-menu and utility tests passed 19/19;
  module-registry/package-contract tests passed 22/22; and the complete unit
  suite passed 294/294.

### Phase 1 completion gate

- [x] Definition validation, color fallback, snapshot immutability, enum use,
      and selection context are deterministic without a live DF runtime.
- [x] Every new class and method has LuaDoc documentation.

## Phase 2 - Implement widget and map-tile registration

- [x] Add `context_menu/registration.lua` as the owner of public widget
      registrations and registration lifecycle.
- [x] Preserve tooltip-like weak widget ownership so registration does not
      keep a removed widget alive.
- [x] Make repeated registration of the same widget replace its stored
      validated definition without creating a duplicate registration,
      sequence entry, discovery loop, or input hook.
- [x] Preserve the widget's numeric registration identity across direct
      re-registration and update.
- [x] Define registration idempotence in terms of ownership and side effects;
      do not require table identity or structural equality comparisons between
      caller definitions.
- [x] Implement explicit widget update and unregister behavior.
- [x] Validate widget re-registration and update inputs before mutation so a
      failed definition replacement leaves the prior registration intact.
- [x] Resolve the widget's eligible current root dynamically instead of
      retaining a stale screen or overlay root.
- [x] Separate attachment/root discovery from target eligibility so a hidden
      or inactive child can still identify the Lua screen whose reversible
      input wrapper must remain installed.
- [x] Reject detached, hidden, inactive, disabled-overlay, foreign-root, or
      otherwise ineligible widget registrations at target-resolution time.
- [x] Add `context_menu/map_target.lua` with exact copied signed-16 x/y/z
      coordinates, collision-free 48-bit packed integer index keys, weak owner
      semantics, and disposable registration handles.
- [x] Keep a map registration's numeric identity across atomic updates without
      retaining its disposable handle strongly in an open session.
- [x] For duplicate map coordinates, choose the currently eligible registration
      with the greatest original registration sequence. Atomic update retains
      that sequence even when it moves an older handle onto an occupied tile.
- [x] Implement atomic map-handle updates for position and definition.
- [x] Ensure a failed update leaves the prior map registration intact.
- [x] Automatically remove map registrations when their weak owner or handle
      is no longer live.
- [x] Resolve map-owner eligibility through the shared tooltip-style root
      resolver for every target sample and open-session validation. A live owner
      in a hidden, inactive, disabled, unpresented, or wrong-screen root is not
      eligible but may become eligible again without re-registration.
- [x] Start registration-gated input/root discovery when at least one live
      registration exists, regardless of its current visibility, attachment,
      overlay enablement, or presentation eligibility.
- [x] Add `context_menu/root_discovery.lua` with one reload-safe,
      registration-gated frame callback chain for discovering attachment and
      root changes that cannot be observed from an installed input hook.
- [x] Protect every discovery callback with `xpcall(..., debug.traceback)`.
      Record and print the failure, stop the owned callback chain, and expose a
      documented failure callback that the service will use to close active
      presentation and disable the generation.
- [x] Ensure root discovery reads only the live registered widget/map owners
      and does not scan unrelated views or world state.
- [x] Evaluate target eligibility dynamically for each input sample and render.
- [x] Keep enough discovery active to observe a temporarily hidden, detached,
      disabled, or unpresented registration becoming eligible again.
- [x] Stop discovery and release hooks only when the final live registration
      disappears and no menu is open.
- [x] Add focused registration tests for collection, detach, visibility,
      root changes, disabled overlays, duplicate map positions, failed updates,
      update-stable duplicate precedence, map-owner eligibility, discovery
      failure containment, explicit unregister, and reload generation changes.

### Phase 2 implementation record

- `dwarfui/context_menu/registration.lua` owns weak widget registration,
  idempotent definition replacement, explicit update/unregister operations,
  shared widget/map numeric identity allocation, attachment-only root
  discovery, strict dynamic eligibility, and destructive generation reload.
- `dwarfui/context_menu/map_target.lua` owns copied signed-16 DF coordinates,
  exact collision-free 48-bit packed integer index keys, weak owners and
  handles, atomic position-plus-definition updates, stable original duplicate
  precedence, dynamic shared-root eligibility, and automatic dead-owner/handle
  pruning.
- `dwarfui/context_menu/root_discovery.lua` owns one registration-gated
  next-frame callback chain. Demand, scheduling, discovery, root observers,
  idle notification, and failure notification run behind traceback boundaries;
  a failure is printed, recorded, and disables the owning registration
  generation.
- Attachment discovery deliberately ignores visibility, activity, overlay
  enablement, presentation, and layout. Target resolution separately uses the
  strict shared tooltip root resolver on every request, allowing temporarily
  ineligible registrations to recover without re-registration.
- The `dwarfui reload` command shuts down the loaded context-menu registration
  manager before clearing script environments. The process singleton also
  shuts down a still-callable prior manager during direct module reload, and
  stale manager references destructively retire their weak registrations and
  cannot accept new mutations.
- The new modules are registered in dependency order and included in the
  package-contract inventory. Registration has no screen, view attachment, or
  input-consumption path; its root observer is only the seam through which a
  later service may install the explicitly permitted reversible input wrapper.
- Verification on 2026-07-30: focused root-discovery, map-target, and
  registration tests passed 26/26; focused registration, reload-command,
  module-registry, and package-contract tests passed 50/50; the complete unit
  suite passed 320/320; Lua 5.4.6 syntax passed for 31 production and 50
  test/support files; unit-test naming and `git diff --check` passed. The
  non-live build passed syntax; live reload was not run because no
  `dfhack-run.exe` was configured.

### Phase 2 completion gate

- [x] Widget and map registrations have weak, reload-safe, explicitly tested
      ownership and cleanup; registration data itself is never preserved across
      reload.
- [x] Registration alone does not open a screen, consume input, attach views,
      or mutate consumer layout or presentation. It may cause the service to
      install the explicitly permitted reversible input-method wrapper.

## Phase 3 - Implement target detection and anchor capture

- [x] Add `context_menu/input_sample.lua` to construct one immutable
      synchronous sample for each candidate opening input call.
- [x] Read `dfhack.screen.getMousePos()` exactly once for the sample's
      screen/interface coordinate.
- [x] When live map registrations exist, read `dfhack.gui.getMousePos()`
      exactly once for the sample's exact map coordinate.
- [x] Use the same input sample for widget hit testing, DwarfUI blocker
      resolution, exact map lookup, target precedence, and anchor capture.
- [x] Do not reuse the asynchronous tooltip pointer-poller state for opening;
      it can be one or more frames older than the input being consumed.
- [x] Add `context_menu/target_detector.lua` and reuse the generic
      `dwarfui/pointer` traversal policies instead of creating a second widget
      hit tester.
- [x] Refactor `dwarfui/pointer.lua` to publish immutable numeric
      `PointerPolicy` and `PointerResultKind` enums and return only named enum
      members.
- [x] Migrate existing pointer consumers, widget attributes, tooltip target
      detection, package fixtures, and focused tests to named pointer enum
      members; remove string policy/result comparisons from production and
      tests.
- [x] Update module-registry and package-contract expectations for the shared
      pointer module's new enum exports.
- [x] Move `dwarfui/pointer` before `dwarfui/widget_extensions` in module
      registry dependency order so pointer attributes can install named enum
      defaults in a fresh DFHack process without literal strings or an implicit
      out-of-order load.
- [x] Traverse overlapping UI descendants in reverse render order.
- [x] Preserve the existing target, pass, block, and none behavior through the
      corresponding named `PointerPolicy` members.
- [x] Across distinct eligible DwarfUI roots, choose the registered widget with
      the latest original registration sequence, matching the tooltip detector.
- [x] If at least one registered widget target is found, ignore blockers from
      other roots. If no registered widget target is found, any blocker
      suppresses map fallback.
- [x] Allow only a currently eligible registered widget to resolve as a widget
      context-menu target.
- [x] Suppress map fallback when pointer traversal reports a blocking UI
      region in a traversed DwarfUI root.
- [x] Attempt exact same-z map lookup only after widget resolution reports a
      true miss rather than a block and the synchronous sample contains a
      valid map coordinate.
- [x] Filter exact map candidates through their current weak-owner root
      eligibility before applying duplicate-coordinate precedence.
- [x] Give a widget target precedence over a map target at the same screen
      position.
- [x] Capture the opening pointer position as a copied
      `SCREEN_POSITION` anchor for a widget target.
- [x] Capture the registered copied world coordinate as a `MAP_TILE` anchor for
      a map target.
- [x] Preserve stable target identity in the selection context and retain
      source widgets, map owners, and roots only through the session's defined
      weak-reference mechanism.
- [x] Do not infer a map target from a projected marker, label, or proximity;
      resolve the exact world tile under the pointer.
- [x] Add focused tests for within-root overlap, cross-root overlap and
      registration order, clipping, target ownership, blockers within and
      across roots, DwarfUI-over-map precedence, exact same-z matching,
      duplicate map entries before and after atomic updates, map-owner
      visibility/presentation/current-screen eligibility, both anchor
      descriptor types, and pointer enum migration.

### Phase 3 implementation record

- `dwarfui/context_menu/input_sample.lua` captures the screen/interface
  pointer exactly once per opening attempt and conditionally captures one exact
  map coordinate only while map registrations demand it. The resulting scalar
  snapshot is immutable and independent of the asynchronous tooltip poller.
- `dwarfui/context_menu/target_detector.lua` applies the shared pointer
  traversal to every currently eligible registered root, selects the latest
  originally registered widget across roots, treats unregistered controls and
  explicit blockers as map-fallback blockers, and consults exact map
  registration only after a true widget miss.
- Widget detections carry a stable numeric registration identity and a copied
  screen-position anchor. Map detections carry the stable map registration
  identity and a copied registered world-tile anchor; projected markers,
  labels, and proximity do not participate in detection.
- `dwarfui/pointer.lua` now publishes immutable numeric `PointerPolicy` and
  `PointerResultKind` enums. Widget defaults, tooltip detection and rendering,
  the minecart overlay, package fixtures, and focused tests use named enum
  members. The module registry loads pointer before widget extensions.
- The registration manager exposes only currently eligible roots for
  arbitration, including map-owner roots whose blocking UI must suppress map
  fallback. Map duplicate precedence and atomic update sequencing remain
  owned by the exact packed-coordinate registry.
- Verification on 2026-07-30: focused context-menu tests passed 50/50; the
  shared pointer and affected-consumer regression set passed 96/96; the
  complete unit suite passed 330/330; and Lua 5.4.6 syntax passed for 33
  production and 52 test/support files.

### Phase 3 completion gate

- [x] One pointer sample produces exactly one eligible target, block, or miss.
- [x] UI targets produce fixed screen anchors and map targets produce copied
      world anchors.
- [x] A blocking region in a traversed DwarfUI root cannot accidentally expose
      a map context menu behind that DwarfUI region.

## Phase 4 - Implement the service and opening hooks

- [x] Add `context_menu/service.lua` as the single process-wide owner of the
      active session, hook generation, and diagnostics.
- [x] Enforce at most one active context menu process-wide.
- [x] Expose one documented service transition for opening, closing, and
      selecting; input surfaces must not mutate session state independently.
- [x] Define an injected presentation-factory boundary between the service and
      the concrete ZScreen. Use a fake presenter for Phase 4 service/hook tests;
      do not claim production opening integration until Phase 5 supplies and
      wires the real screen/renderer factory.
- [x] Add `context_menu/input_hook.lua` with reload-safe hook ownership,
      predecessor preservation, exact restoration, and foreign-wrapper safety.
- [x] Match the tooltip render hook's wrapper contract: restore the exact
      predecessor only when DwarfUI's trampoline is still outermost; never
      overwrite a foreign outer wrapper; invalidate and diagnose an inert
      superseded trampoline that remains inside a foreign chain.
- [x] Wrap context-menu hook logic in `xpcall(..., debug.traceback)`. Track
      `last_error`, `last_failure`, `failure_count`, and
      `disabled_generation`, and report failures through `dfhack.printerr`,
      matching the tooltip hook's diagnostic style.
- [x] Wire root discovery's protected failure callback into the same service
      diagnostics and disabled-generation transition. A discovery failure stops
      discovery, closes any active menu, safely releases owned hooks where
      possible, and leaves remaining trampolines transparent until reload.
- [x] Intercept native viewscreen input at
      `plugins.overlay.feed_viewscreen_widgets()` before delegating to its
      predecessor.
- [x] Intercept Lua-screen input through the explicitly permitted reversible
      wrapper around the owning screen instance's effective `onInput()` method.
- [x] Install Lua-screen hooks on attached roots that own live registrations;
      current target visibility or presentation eligibility must not be
      required to retain discovery of that root.
- [x] Restrict opening hooks to actionable opening-target resolution. They do
      not process interaction for an already-visible context-menu screen.
- [x] Delegate `_MOUSE_R_DOWN`-only input unchanged to the predecessor; do not
      sample, resolve, consume, or retain an opening target from that event.
- [x] On `_MOUSE_R`, construct the synchronous input sample, resolve and
      validate the current target and anchor, consume the complete input table,
      and open from that same call.
- [x] If `_MOUSE_R_DOWN` and `_MOUSE_R` arrive together, resolve, consume, and
      open exactly once from `_MOUSE_R` in that one input call; the complete
      table is consumed.
- [x] If `_MOUSE_R` resolves a miss or blocker without a map target, delegate
      the original unmodified key table to the predecessor and preserve its
      exact return values.
- [x] For a Lua-screen opening hook, return handled without invoking the
      predecessor when the event opens a menu. Otherwise invoke the predecessor
      exactly once with the original unmodified table.
- [x] For a native opening hook, return handled for the complete original input
      table when the event opens a menu. Otherwise delegate the original table
      through the normal overlay/native chain.
- [x] Do not partition, clone, or reinject a handled input table. Additional
      keys that share a handled context-menu event are consumed with it.
- [x] If sampling or target resolution fails before a registered target is
      established, own no keys: delegate the original table unchanged, record
      diagnostics, and disable context-menu handling for the generation.
- [x] If presentation fails after a registered target is established,
      atomically roll back any partial screen/session, consume the complete
      opening input table, record diagnostics, and disable context-menu handling
      for the generation.
- [x] Once disabled, opening hooks become transparent predecessor delegates
      until reload resets the modules and drops all context-menu state.
- [x] Close the session before invoking `on_select`, and prevent handler errors
      from restoring or duplicating the closed session.
- [x] Report handler errors through one documented protected-call boundary
      owned by the service.
- [x] Add focused service and hook tests for native and Lua seams,
      `_MOUSE_R_DOWN` transparency, actionable and coalesced `_MOUSE_R`,
      complete-event consumption, unchanged irrelevant-event delegation, misses,
      blockers, predecessor return preservation, internal failure
      rollback/disablement, discovery failure integration, destructive reload
      reset, exact outermost restoration, inert superseded trampolines, and
      foreign wrappers.

### Phase 4 implementation record

- `dwarfui/context_menu/service.lua` owns the sole session and the protected
  open, close, and select transitions. It constructs a hidden presentation
  controller through an injected factory and calls `show()` only after the
  controller is service-owned, allowing failed presentation to close both the
  controller and session before the generation becomes transparent.
- The production singleton remains inactive until the concrete presentation
  factory is installed by the interactive-screen work. This preserves the
  boundary that Phase 4 can validate opening ownership without enabling a
  production path that has no renderer.
- `dwarfui/context_menu/input_hook.lua` wraps native overlay input before its
  predecessor and attached Lua-screen `onInput()` methods reversibly. It owns
  complete input tables, preserves exact predecessor returns on delegation,
  restores only outermost owned methods, and retains only diagnostic metadata
  for trampolines trapped beneath foreign wrappers.
- Internal resolution, discovery, hook, presentation, and cleanup failures
  close all reachable state, stop discovery and owned hooks, record service and
  hook diagnostics, and leave the generation transparent. Consumer
  `on_select` failures are reported after close without disabling later menus.
- Wrapper-chain inspection is shared by the context-menu and tooltip hooks
  through `dwarfui/utils/function_chain.lua`. A focused target-detector fixture
  now retains its weak map handle explicitly so garbage collection cannot turn
  its intended blocker assertion into a nondeterministic miss.
- Verification on 2026-07-30: focused context-menu tests passed 67/67; the
  complete unit suite passed 349/349; Lua 5.4.6 syntax passed for 36 production
  and 54 test/support files; the offline build passed; and `git diff --check`
  reported no whitespace errors.

### Phase 4 completion gate

- [x] The service is the sole authority for session state.
- [x] Each actionable opening input table opens at most once and is consumed in
      full; an irrelevant table is delegated unchanged exactly once.
- [x] A miss is behaviorally transparent to the predecessor.
- [x] No mouse-down latch or post-dismissal suppression state exists unless the
      Phase 0 seam evidence required an explicit checklist revision.
- [x] An internal failure cannot leave a partial session, visible screen, active
      handler, or input-trapping generation behind.

## Phase 5 - Implement the interactive menu screen and renderer

- [ ] Add `context_menu/screen.lua` as a short-lived `gui.ZScreen` host owned
      by one open session.
- [ ] Add the concrete screen/renderer presentation factory, wire it into the
      service, and only then enable the facade's production opening path.
- [ ] While its ZScreen is current, make `ContextMenuScreen:onInput()` the
      context menu's physical input surface for interaction and dismissal; it
      delegates every state transition to the service. A later ZScreen above it
      receives input normally.
- [ ] Do not call `gui.ZScreen:onInput()` for menu-owned dispatch because it
      performs its own Escape/right-click dismissal. Classify the complete
      event, route it through the service and native Window/List in the locked
      priority order, and keep every close/select transition service-owned.
- [ ] Configure `initial_pause=false`, `force_pause=false`,
      `pass_pause=false`, `pass_movement_keys=false`,
      `pass_mouse_clicks=false`, and `defocusable=false`. The context-menu
      screen explicitly delegates complete irrelevant input tables itself.
- [ ] Render the parent first and the context menu last so the menu is the
      topmost final paint while its ZScreen is current. If another ZScreen is
      above it, preserve normal screen-stack paint order.
- [ ] Determine event relevance, pointer-inside-Window state, and ownership
      before entering protected Window/List or service dispatch. If dispatch
      then raises, the already-classified relevant table remains consumed.
- [ ] Before Window/List dispatch, give `LEAVESCREEN` and actionable `_MOUSE_R`
      first priority and close once. Next, close once for actionable
      `_MOUSE_L` outside the complete Window. These dismissal actions win over
      every List key coalesced into the same table.
- [ ] After dismissal checks, dispatch the original intact table through the
      native Window/List. Let the native implementation choose at most one
      transition when several remaining List-owned keys coexist.
- [ ] If Window/List handles the table, consume it in full. If it does not
      handle actionable `_MOUSE_L` inside the complete Window, consume the
      table as a no-op so frame, title, padding, and empty body clicks cannot
      reach backing UI. Otherwise send the original table unchanged to the
      parent exactly once with `sendInputToParent(keys)`.
- [ ] Treat list navigation, list paging, list selection, wheel input inside
      the complete Window, Escape, actionable right-click, and actionable
      left-click inside or outside the Window as context-menu-relevant. A table
      containing only pause, map movement, or other irrelevant inputs passes to
      the parent even while the menu is open.
- [ ] Do not offer wheel-only input outside the complete Window to the List;
      delegate that original table unchanged. Wheel input inside the Window may
      scroll the List and consumes the complete table when handled.
- [ ] A table containing any handled Window/List or service action is consumed
      in full even when it also contains pause, movement, or another otherwise
      irrelevant key.
- [ ] Do not forward a handled table containing Escape, right-click, outside
      left-click, entry selection, or another context-menu action.
- [ ] On Escape, request one service-owned close and consume the complete input
      table containing `LEAVESCREEN`.
- [ ] On a second actionable `_MOUSE_R`, request one service-owned close,
      consume the complete input table, and never resolve or open an underlying
      target.
- [ ] On an outside actionable `_MOUSE_L`, request one service-owned close and
      consume the complete input table.
- [ ] On an entry `_MOUSE_L`, consume the complete input table and invoke
      selection exactly once through the service's close-before-callback
      transition.
- [ ] `_MOUSE_L_DOWN` or `_MOUSE_R_DOWN` alone is irrelevant: it neither selects
      nor dismisses and its original input table is delegated unchanged. If a
      down flag and its actionable click are coalesced, act exactly once from
      the actionable click and consume the complete table.
- [ ] Route keyboard selection through that same service transition.
- [ ] Add `context_menu/renderer.lua` with a `widgets.Window` using
      `gui.FRAME_INTERIOR`, `draggable=false`, `resizable=false`, the optional
      title as its native frame title, and a selectable, scrollable
      `widgets.List` filling its content area.
- [ ] Create a per-menu copy of the `gui.FRAME_INTERIOR` style whose frame and
      title pens use the resolved menu foreground/background, and set the
      Window body background from the same resolved menu background. Never
      mutate the shared DFHack frame style.
- [ ] Treat the complete Window frame and body as context-owned pointer space.
      Rely on native Window/List hit testing and interaction; do not add
      independent title, border, or padding actions.
- [ ] Size preferred width from the longest title or entry label plus frame
      padding, but cap final width to the available interface width.
- [ ] Deterministically right-truncate title and entry text that exceeds the
      capped content width, using an ellipsis when at least four content cells
      are available and direct clipping on narrower interfaces.
- [ ] Cap height to the available screen space and enable wheel and keyboard
      scrolling when entries do not fit.
- [ ] Fill each complete entry row with its resolved background rather than
      coloring only the label cells.
- [ ] Paint each entry with its resolved foreground and background.
- [ ] Define deterministic selected and hovered pens that preserve readable
      contrast when entries use custom colors.
- [ ] Keep the native Window title nonselectable and omit any automatic
      list-count suffix.
- [ ] Support keyboard navigation and selection, left-click selection, and
      wheel scrolling.
- [ ] Clamp the final menu frame to every screen edge without changing the
      stored source anchor.
- [ ] Recompute layout after interface dimension changes.
- [ ] Dismiss the screen exactly once on selection, explicit close, source
      invalidation, reload, world unload, or owner removal.
- [ ] Wrap open-screen routing, layout, projection, and rendering boundaries in
      protected calls. On internal failure, report diagnostics, dismiss the
      Window/ZScreen, clear the session, disable context-menu handling for the
      generation, and consume the complete table if a context-menu action had
      already been established. Failures before ownership is established
      delegate the original table unchanged.
- [ ] Add focused renderer and screen tests for an absent title, long labels,
      horizontal truncation, custom pens, selection pens, scrolling, every
      screen edge, small interfaces, resize, native Window/List composition,
      whole-event ownership, dismissal-over-List priority for mixed relevant
      keys, irrelevant pause/movement/keyboard delegation, inside-chrome
      click consumption, outside-wheel delegation, down-only and coalesced
      mouse flags, color-domain and frame-style isolation, internal failure
      containment, and all close paths.

### Phase 5 completion gate

- [ ] While current, the menu is readable, topmost, navigable, and fully
      interactive with both default and entry-specific colors; a later ZScreen
      can naturally appear and receive input above it.
- [ ] Screen ownership does not pause the game or pass menu-owned input to the
      parent.
- [ ] While current, the visible ZScreen rather than an underlying opening hook
      owns every context-menu interaction.
- [ ] Every dismissal path destroys one open screen and one session.

## Phase 6 - Implement screen-position and map-tile placement

- [ ] Place the preferred menu frame with its top-left cell at the source
      anchor.
- [ ] If the preferred frame overflows right or bottom, try the corresponding
      left-of-anchor or above-anchor placement before final clamping.
- [ ] Apply deterministic horizontal and vertical placement independently so
      corner behavior is testable.
- [ ] Keep a widget menu's captured screen anchor fixed while its source
      widget moves or rerenders.
- [ ] Re-clamp a widget menu after screen-size changes without changing its
      captured anchor.
- [ ] For map-tile sessions, retain a copied x/y/z world coordinate and
      re-resolve the current dwarfmode viewport before every render.
- [ ] Use the active DFHack viewport helper for text-mode world-to-screen
      projection.
- [ ] Use the active Premium viewport corner, interface dimensions, and zoom
      factor for graphics-mode projection.
- [ ] Use the projection helper's canonical interface cell for the exact map
      tile as the map menu anchor in both text and Premium modes.
- [ ] Keep the projected anchor and menu frame in one interface coordinate
      space.
- [ ] Reproject after camera panning, zoom, interface resize, and other
      viewport changes.
- [ ] Close and consume no additional input when the tile becomes offscreen,
      changes away from the displayed z-level, the world unloads, or the
      owning registration becomes invalid.
- [ ] Close without invoking a handler when the map registration's owner root
      becomes hidden, inactive, disabled, or absent from the menu's still-live
      presented parent/root chain. Merely placing a later ZScreen above the
      context menu does not invalidate the owner root.
- [ ] Do not pin an offscreen map menu to the nearest screen edge; it would no
      longer appear attached to its source tile.
- [ ] Do not change viewport, cursor, game pause, or native map state merely to
      keep the menu visible.
- [ ] Add shared `dwarfui/map_projection.lua` and migrate the existing
      minecart projection to it in the same change, giving the helper both
      minecart and context-menu production consumers without duplicating
      graphics-mode math.
- [ ] Add unit tests proving a screen-position anchor remains fixed.
- [ ] Add unit tests proving a map anchor moves after viewport changes and
      closes for offscreen, wrong-z, dead-owner, ineligible or unpresented
      owner-root, and world-unload cases.
- [ ] Add text-mode and Premium projection coverage for map edges, zoom,
      interface resize, preferred placement, left/above fallback, every
      fallback combination, and final clamping.

### Phase 6 completion gate

- [ ] Widget context menus remain at their captured screen positions.
- [ ] Map context menus visibly track their world tiles every render.
- [ ] Projection invalidation closes cleanly without stale screens, map
      mutation, or input side effects.

## Phase 7 - Integrate lifecycle, reload, registry, packaging, and docs

- [ ] Make module reload invalidate stale callbacks, registrations, screens,
      and active render/input ownership from the prior generation.
- [ ] Treat reload as a complete context-menu reset: close the active screen,
      remove all hooks owned by the generation when safely restorable, drop all
      widget/map registrations, numeric registration identities, callbacks,
      service diagnostics, and disabled-generation state, and require explicit
      re-registration afterward. Retain only minimal hook-chain metadata when
      the tooltip-style foreign-wrapper contract makes exact removal
      impossible.
- [ ] Apply the tooltip hook cleanup contract: restore only an outermost hook
      owned by this generation, never overwrite a foreign wrapper installed
      after DwarfUI, and make any superseded trampoline retained inside that
      foreign chain inert and visible in diagnostics.
- [ ] Clear the active menu and registrations on world unload and DwarfUI
      teardown.
- [ ] Add every reload-managed module to
      `dwarfui/module_registry.lua` in dependency order.
- [ ] Update module-registry tests for load and environment-clear order.
- [ ] Update package-contract tests for all
      `dwarfui/context_menu/` production modules.
- [ ] Update build/package verification so nested production and test
      directories are discovered correctly.
- [ ] Document the public registration, update, unregister, map-handle,
      definition, color-fallback, selection-context, close, and anchoring
      contracts in the project README or a dedicated guide.
- [ ] Document accepted `COLOR_BLACK`..`COLOR_WHITE` display colors, menu
      frame/title/body color application, update-stable duplicate map
      precedence, map-handle lifetime, and map-owner root eligibility.
- [ ] Document that the initial public API has no programmatic replacement
      operation and that a right-click while open only closes.
- [ ] Include examples for a widget screen-position menu and an exact map-tile
      menu.
- [ ] Document that selection and outside left-click both close and consume.
- [ ] Link `Docs/project.todo` to this checklist without marking the project
      item complete before every definition-of-done gate passes.

### Phase 7 completion gate

- [ ] Reload and teardown leave no stale screen, active hook ownership,
      registration, discovery callback, or other callback. A superseded inert
      trampoline may remain only when an opaque foreign outer wrapper prevents
      exact restoration, matching the tooltip hook contract, and must be
      reported by diagnostics.
- [ ] Registry order, package contents, public documentation, and both anchor
      examples match the implemented API.

## Phase 8 - Add native component and interaction coverage

- [ ] Add a DwarfSpec component test for a registered DwarfUI overlay control
      whose context menu opens at the captured screen position.
- [ ] Before executing any Phase 8 live test, run a preliminary offline build
      and expanded/archive package verification, install that exact package in
      the configured DFHack instance, and start a fresh DF/DFHack process. This
      is the first pointer-enum live acceptance; `dwarfui reload` is not a
      substitute for the required fresh process.
- [ ] Verify the live script lookup and module registry resolve the newly
      installed package before interpreting any component-test result.
- [ ] Right-click the actual rendered control through the production input
      boundary.
- [ ] Prove the opening hook samples the screen pointer synchronously and does
      not depend on the tooltip poller's previous-frame state.
- [ ] Prove `_MOUSE_R_DOWN` alone follows the audited transparent predecessor
      behavior and does not open a context menu.
- [ ] Prove the backing UI does not receive the owned `_MOUSE_R` key when it
      opens the menu.
- [ ] Prove an outside left-click closes and does not reach backing UI.
- [ ] Reopen and prove a second right-click closes and does not reopen or reach
      backing UI.
- [ ] Prove a mixed input table containing a handled context-menu click is
      consumed in full and does not deliver any additional key to backing UI.
- [ ] Prove mixed relevant-key priority: Escape or right-click closes instead of
      navigating or selecting, and an outside left-click closes instead of
      activating a coalesced List selection.
- [ ] Reopen and prove Escape closes and is consumed.
- [ ] Reopen, select an entry with a real left-click, and prove it closes,
      consumes, calls the handler once, and supplies a non-nil live `source` in
      the selection context.
- [ ] Reopen, select an entry with keyboard input, and prove it closes,
      consumes, calls the handler once, and supplies the same guaranteed-live
      selection-context contract.
- [ ] Add a Lua `gui.ZScreen` component case that opens through its instance
      input seam rather than the native overlay seam.
- [ ] Add a map-tile component case against a real visible same-z map tile.
- [ ] Prove opening the map menu suppresses native map right-click behavior.
- [ ] Move the map camera while the menu remains open and prove the menu moves
      with the same source tile.
- [ ] Prove list navigation consumes its complete event table. Prove a separate
      map-movement event table still moves the camera exactly once.
- [ ] Prove a left-click on title, frame, padding, or empty body is consumed
      without an action or backing-UI click, and prove a wheel-only event outside
      the Window is delegated unchanged.
- [ ] Prove input tables containing only pause or irrelevant keyboard input pass
      through unchanged for both anchor kinds.
- [ ] Change z-level or pan the source tile outside the visible viewport and
      prove the menu closes cleanly.
- [ ] Exercise custom title, menu colors, per-entry overrides, long lists,
      scrolling, and selection in the mounted production renderer. Verify the
      menu-level colors on the native frame, title, body, and fallback entry
      rows without modifying another Window's shared frame style.
- [ ] Hide, disable, bury, and restore a live map registration's owner root and
      prove ineligible states cannot open or retain a menu while restoration
      requires no re-registration.
- [ ] Show a separate test ZScreen above an open context menu and prove the
      upper screen naturally receives input and paints last without forced
      raising, focus trapping, or context-menu dismissal.
- [ ] In controlled failure cases, prove an internal
      discovery/hook/screen/render failure closes partial presentation,
      consumes the complete table after ownership is established, delegates it
      unchanged before ownership is established, records diagnostics, stops
      owned discovery callbacks, and leaves the generation transparent until
      reload.
- [ ] Prove an `on_select` error is reported after the menu closes, invokes the
      handler only once, and does not disable subsequent context menus.
- [ ] Hide and restore a registered widget without re-registering it and prove
      discovery observes its return to eligibility.
- [ ] Mutate a caller-owned definition after registration and prove neither
      the next opening nor an already-open menu changes.
- [ ] In a controlled disposable case, invalidate a weak source while its menu
      is open and prove the menu closes without invoking a handler.
- [ ] Snapshot and restore pointer, viewport, z-level, pause state, screen
      stack, overlay state, and any test-owned registrations even when an
      assertion fails.
- [ ] Require DwarfSpec `cleanup_confirmed=true` and independently inspect
      retained screens, active hooks, registrations, discovery callbacks, and
      other callbacks.

### Phase 8 completion gate

- [ ] Production widget and map registrations open through their real input
      seams and consume opening, closing, and selection input.
- [ ] Opening hooks only open; while current, the visible production ZScreen
      owns its interactions and dismissals, and later ZScreens retain normal
      screen-stack priority.
- [ ] The screen-position anchor is fixed and the map-tile anchor tracks camera
      movement in the mounted production system.
- [ ] Cleanup is confirmed with no retained UI, active hooks, registrations, or
      changed game state, except for any inert diagnosed trampoline retained
      inside an opaque foreign wrapper under the tooltip hook contract.

## Phase 9 - Complete verification and live acceptance

- [ ] Run the focused definition, registration, root-discovery, input-sample,
      detector, pointer-enum, hook, renderer, screen, service, and projection
      unit specs through `./tools/Run-UnitTests.ps1`.
- [ ] Run the existing shared pointer, tooltip target-detector, and affected
      consumer specs as a focused regression set for the enum migration.
- [ ] Run the focused context-menu DwarfSpec files through
      `./tools/Run-AutomationTests.ps1`, which remains a direct
      `dwarfspec run` pass-through.
- [ ] Run the complete standalone unit suite.
- [ ] Run the complete DwarfSpec suite.
- [ ] Run `./tools/Check-LuaSyntax.ps1 -IncludeTests`.
- [ ] Run the offline build through `./tools/Build.ps1` without claiming live
      runtime evidence.
- [ ] Run expanded and archive package verification through the scripts under
      `./tools/`.
- [ ] Run `git diff --check`.
- [ ] Reinstall the final verified package in the configured live DFHack
      instance and restart DF/DFHack before final manual acceptance. The earlier
      Phase 8 fresh-process gate remains the first pointer-enum live acceptance.
- [ ] Register disposable live test consumers and open a menu, run
      `dwarfui reload`, prove registrations and presentation were dropped, and
      explicitly re-register the live test consumers before continuing
      interaction acceptance.
- [ ] Confirm the live module registry contains one coherent context-menu
      generation, no stale screen, active hook ownership, or discovery callback,
      and no undiagnosed inert trampoline.
- [ ] Manually right-click a registered UI control and confirm the menu opens
      at the click's screen position.
- [ ] Manually confirm opening, outside left-click closing, second-right-click
      closing, Escape, mouse selection, and keyboard selection are consumed
      and do not activate backing UI.
- [ ] Manually right-click a registered map tile and confirm the menu follows
      map panning and zoom.
- [ ] Manually confirm map-tile invalidation on viewport exit and z-level
      change.
- [ ] Review DFHack console output and logs after interaction, reload, world
      unload, and teardown.
- [ ] Record focused unit, complete unit, focused DwarfSpec, complete
      DwarfSpec, syntax, build, package, registry, cleanup, and manual visual
      evidence separately.

### Phase 9 completion gate

- [ ] All automated suites, syntax checks, builds, packages, and whitespace
      checks pass.
- [ ] Live DFHack proves both placement modes and all required consumption and
      closure behavior without Lua errors or stale lifecycle state.
- [ ] No automated registration or cleanup result is reported as visual live
      success.

## Definition of done

- [ ] A caller can register a validated `ContextMenuDefinition` against a UI
      widget or exact map tile.
- [ ] Optional title, menu foreground/background defaults, entry labels,
      entry handlers, and independent entry color overrides behave as
      documented.
- [ ] Every `fg` and `bg` value is an integer in the inclusive
      `COLOR_BLACK..COLOR_WHITE` (`0..15`) domain; `COLOR_RESET` and every
      other unsupported value are rejected before registration state changes.
- [ ] Resolved menu colors apply to the native frame, optional title, and body;
      independently resolved entry colors fill their complete rows without
      mutating shared DFHack frame styles.
- [ ] Registration and update operations store validated DwarfUI-owned
      definition copies; later caller table mutation does not bypass
      validation or alter registered/open menus.
- [ ] Widget menus open at the captured screen position and remain fixed there
      while open.
- [ ] Preferred, left/above fallback, and final-clamped placement are
      deterministic for both anchor kinds.
- [ ] Oversized titles and labels are deterministically truncated so the final
      menu width never exceeds the available interface width.
- [ ] Map menus remain attached to their projected world tiles as the camera
      moves and close when the tile is no longer projectable.
- [ ] A map registration can open or remain open only while its weak owner
      resolves to a visible, active, enabled DwarfUI root in the menu's
      currently presented parent/root chain; restoration can make it eligible
      again without re-registration.
- [ ] Opening target detection uses one synchronous screen/map input sample,
      never stale tooltip polling state.
- [ ] Every actionable `_MOUSE_R` that opens a menu is consumed along with a
      coalesced `_MOUSE_R_DOWN` and every other field in the same table;
      down-only input does not open or create retained state.
- [ ] Escape, a second right-click, and an outside left-click close and consume
      without leaking input.
- [ ] Mouse or keyboard item selection closes first, consumes, and invokes the
      selected handler exactly once.
- [ ] Every entry handler is an actual Lua function; callable non-function
      values are rejected during definition validation.
- [ ] Every invoked handler receives a selection context with a non-nil live
      `source`; loss of the weak source closes the menu without invoking it.
- [ ] Context-menu input routing consumes the complete table when it handles a
      relevant action and delegates an irrelevant table unchanged exactly once.
- [ ] Mixed relevant input tables follow one deterministic priority: Escape or
      actionable right-click dismissal, outside actionable left-click
      dismissal, then at most one native Window/List action.
- [ ] Actionable left-clicks anywhere inside the complete Window are consumed,
      including otherwise-unhandled frame, title, padding, and empty-body
      clicks; wheel-only input outside the Window is delegated unchanged.
- [ ] Internal failures close partial presentation, disable context handling
      transparently for the generation, and report diagnostics; consumer
      handler failures close and report without disabling the system.
- [ ] Discovery failures stop their owned callback chain and enter the same
      diagnosed, transparent disabled-generation state as hook, routing,
      layout, projection, and rendering failures.
- [ ] While it is the current top screen, the visible context-menu ZScreen owns
      menu interaction and underlying hooks only open menus. A later ZScreen
      retains normal DFHack input and paint priority above it.
- [ ] Temporarily ineligible registrations can become eligible again without
      re-registration.
- [ ] UI targets, blocking regions, and map targets obey deterministic
      precedence.
- [ ] Duplicate map registrations at one coordinate use greatest original
      registration sequence among currently eligible candidates; atomic
      updates retain that sequence even when moving onto an occupied tile.
- [ ] Open sessions identify their source by numeric registration identity and
      do not strongly retain a widget, owner, or disposable map handle.
- [ ] Shared pointer policies and results use immutable numeric enums in all
      production consumers and tests.
- [ ] Only one menu is open process-wide.
- [ ] A right-click while open only closes and cannot replace the menu.
- [ ] All context-menu-owned source and focused test files live in their
      dedicated directories.
- [ ] Reload, world unload, owner removal, and teardown leave no stale screen,
      active hook ownership, registration, discovery callback, or other owned
      callback; opaque foreign-wrapper chains may retain only inert diagnosed
      trampolines under the tooltip hook contract.
- [ ] Reload drops every context-menu registration, identity, callback,
      session, service diagnostic, and disabled-generation flag; continued use
      requires explicit re-registration. Minimal inert-hook metadata is the
      sole exception.
- [ ] All new and modified classes and methods have LuaDoc comments.
- [ ] Unit, component, syntax, build, package, live registration, cleanup, and
      manual visual evidence are complete and reported without conflation.

